    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_28ce266b6bf29c3dc840f57fc9f95c9fce3f10abeb0d2a99aad43558b291bb05",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For a nonprincipal character under the stated real-part condition, the proof uses Mellin inversion, moves the integration line left, records the resulting coefficient-sum approximation, and then applies character orthogonality. Conceptually, this sequence changes the form of the L-value so that averaging over characters can act through a coefficient relation. The gain is compatibility with the next averaging step; the loss is that the damaged extraction does not establish the exact kernel, contour, truncation length, or error, so neither quantitative strength nor pole contributions can be claimed.",
